   "Per item per pass the fixed-point digit costs FADD, FFMA (with the 2^23 rounding constant), two IMNMX (clamps), LOP3 and ISETP (prefix filter), SHF and LOP3 (digit and bucket flip) before the ATOMS, against LOP3, ISETP, SHF, LOP3 for the radix digit: about 4 instructions more per item per pass. The bracket costs one FMNMX pair per item (per-thread max and min), three redux.sync (T1) and lane 0 stores per warp, and 24 shared loads plus 14 min or max per thread after the barrier that the histogram zeroing needs anyway: no extra barrier. The harness latency kernel at bf16 N 1024 k 16 has 1984 SASS instructions against 656 for B1_idx and 31 BAR.SYNC against 19 (both kernels inline the warm-up call and the chain call).",
   "The committed build (02fdf9dbdc, with the per-pass empty-asm pin on the bracket parameters lo and scale, T24) uses 46 registers at IPT 4 in the latency kernel and 40 in the throughput kernel against 32 for B1_idx (throughput occupancy 6 against 8 blocks per SM), 80 against 47 at IPT 16 (3 against 5), and 255 with 7256 bytes of spill in the latency kernel (332 STL, 1482 LDL, 632-byte stack frame) and 3632 bytes in the throughput kernel at IPT 128, against 248 to 250 with 0 for B1_idx. The pin alone does not bound the per-item state, something still holds about 128 words per thread across the passes at IPT 128, and the spill dominates N 32768: 22127 against 16352 cycles at bf16 k 16, 60986 against 24579 at k 1024.",
   "Tie-class tracking in this build takes the min and max over all candidates of the pass, not over the pivot bucket, so a bf16 tie class inside a larger candidate set is detected one pass late: bf16 at N 1024 k 64 and 256 (2.1 and 2.3 ties at rank k on average in this batch) runs 3 fixed-point passes where 2 would do, 2234.6 against 1877.8 and 2704.3 against 1971.0 for B1_idx's 2 radix passes."
  ],
  "algorithmic": [
   "Where the bracket pass replaces two radix passes by one (small k, the pivot bucket holds exactly the remaining k or a single value), N6 wins in latency: N 1024 k 16 bf16 1726.7 against 1767.3 (2 percent), fp32 1702.9 against 1793.5 (5 percent), N 4096 k 16 bf16 2770.9 against 3369.1 (18 percent), fp32 2810.7 against 3661.6 (23 percent), fp32 N 4096 k 64 3801.6 against 4586.0 (17 percent), k 256 4523.0 against 5050.3 (10 percent), k 1024 5669.1 against 5702.1 (even), and N 32768 k 16 fp32 14538.3 against 15008.4 (3 percent) despite 7.3 KB of spill. The host simulation (sim_passes.md section 2) predicted the pivot bin after one pass at 1.1 to 7.3 items (mean, at most 16) for every random cell with k at most 256 under the thread-max bracket, and the exact exit after the second pass in 97 to 100 percent of the rows, which the GPU confirms where the register footprint does not intervene.",
   "Where ties at the k-th key need the tie-class exit (bf16, 1.4 to 18.6 ties at rank k on average in this batch) the late detection of this build costs a pass and N6 loses at k 64 and above: bf16 N 1024 k 64 2234.6 against 1877.8 (19 percent), k 256 2704.3 against 1971.0 (37 percent), N 4096 k 64 4113.7 against 3837.3 (7 percent), k 256 5538.6 against 4514.4 (23 percent), k 1024 6303.5 against 4688.1 (35 percent). fp32 without ties is 6 to 7 percent behind at N 1024 k 64 and 256 (2017.6 against 1902.3, 2297.9 against 2150.1, the bracket and the heavier pass are not paid back by a saved pass there) and 10 to 17 percent ahead at N 4096 k 64 and 256.",
   "Throughput is behind B1_idx in every cell of this batch, by the register footprint where the latency wins: 13 to 30 percent at N 1024 (occupancy 6 against 8 blocks per SM, 352.7 against 408.4 Gelem/s at bf16 k 16), 5 to 32 percent at N 4096 (3 against 5, 654.6 against 692.4 at bf16 k 16, 633.7 against 694.3 at fp32), 8 to 58 percent at N 32768 (the spill, 423.9 against 460.3 at fp32 k 16, 139.3 against 334.1 at bf16 k 1024). At N 32768 the spilled build is 35 to 148 percent slower in latency at bf16 and 22 to 36 percent at fp32 k 64 to 1024.",
   "The kill criteria of the idea card: (1) one pass plus a finish on at least 90 percent of random and logit-like rows holds in the host simulation for every k at most 256 (100 percent of rows end with at most 16 items in the pivot bin, 97 to 100 percent are exact after the second pass), and fails for k above 256 without a tighter lower bound (217 items in the pivot bin at N 32768 k 4096). (2) the per-item cost of the bin function is about 4 instructions per item per pass over the radix extractor by instruction count, under 10 percent of a pass at IPT 4 (the pass is dependency bound) and about 7 percent of the histogram sweep at IPT 128, argued from the SASS and not from the explore-item-2 swap measurement the idea card specified (verify-B1's note). At the named cell N 1024 k 64 bf16 this build is 19 percent behind B1_idx (2234.6 against 1877.8), the per-bucket build B1_n6b about 9 percent behind by chain. N6 is not killed, its gain is real at small k and at fp32, and its weak spots (late tie exit, register footprint) are implementation, not idea."
  ],
  "negative": [
   "Tracking the key min and max over all candidates of a pass (two atomics on two words) is not enough to exit on a bf16 tie class: the pivot bucket of pass p is a tie class long before the whole candidate set is, so bf16 paid a third pass at k 64 and 256 in every N (plus 19 and 37 percent at N 1024, plus 7 and 23 percent at N 4096 against B1_idx).",
   "Recomputing the fixed-point image per pass from the fp32 value is only cheap if the compiler does recompute it, and the empty-asm pin on lo and scale (T24, in this build) is not enough at IPT 128: 255 registers with 7256 bytes of spill remain against 248 to 250 with 0 for B1_idx, and 46 against 32 registers at IPT 4 and 80 against 47 at IPT 16 cost one to two blocks per SM of throughput occupancy.",
   "The kernel of the superseded card B1_n6_b200_20261025_1 (opt-B1's batch n6b, 13:13 UTC) was built from an uncommitted intermediate of the source between f577f3815a and 02fdf9dbdc: 1960 SASS instructions, 40 registers at IPT 4 and 8572 to 8588 spill bytes at IPT 128, against 1984, 46 and 7256 for the committed source. That kernel cannot be rebuilt. Its N 1024 and 4096 numbers agree with this batch within 2.7 percent latency and 4.4 percent throughput, its N 32768 numbers do not (14 of 44 cells 13 to 35 percent slower in latency and 6 to 50 percent lower in throughput than this build), so the superseded card's findings about 8.6 KB of spill describe a binary without a source. A card's commit must be the HEAD of the tree the adapters were shipped from, and that tree must be clean."
  ],
  "surprises": [
   "Three builds of the same N6 algorithm spilled 1464 to 1508 bytes (the n6a build, commit f577f3815a, batch n6a under state/results/opt-B1, not a card), 8572 to 8588 bytes (the uncommitted intermediate of the superseded card) and 7256 bytes (this build) at IPT 128, with one IMNMX per item and a changed scale expression, then the asm pin and a scatter restructure as the only source differences. Against its own B1_idx control in its own batch the n6a build was 27 percent ahead at bf16 N 32768 k 16 (11983 against 16468) and 43 percent ahead at fp32 (8623 against 15144), where this build is 35 percent behind at bf16 (22127 against 16352) and 3 percent ahead at fp32 (14538 against 15008): the compiler's decision whether to hoist the image across the passes is the largest codegen effect seen in this study so far, and the large-N potential of N6 is a register problem, not an algorithmic one.",
   "The inf_denorm battery pattern caught the degenerate bracket in the n6a build: hi equal to plus infinity and lo to minus infinity give a width that overflows fp32, a zero scale and NaN images for the infinite items. The clamps and the finite nonzero scale cost one instruction per item per pass, and this build passes inf_denorm in all 22 cells including k 1024."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
   "With per-bucket tracking (B1_n6b) does bf16 reach the predicted one-pass exit at N 4096 and 32768 (the simulation says the pivot bin is a single bf16 value in 100 percent of the rows there for k at most 256)? The first pass does not track, so that exit still needs a representable-value test on the pivot bin's value interval, cheap and block-uniform, not yet written.",
   "A tighter lower bound for k above 256 (the minimum over threads of the per-thread second or m-th largest, B4's bound) would extend the one-pass regime to k 256 m, the full bracket leaves 37 to 217 items in the pivot bin at k 1024 and 4096.",
   "What holds about 128 words per thread across the passes at IPT 128 with the pin on lo and scale in place (255 registers, 7256 spill bytes against 248 to 250 and 0 for B1_idx)? ptxas -v and the SASS of this build against B1_idx at N 32768 are the next step, the twiddled key per item and the second image copy for the fallback are the candidates, a two-half item loop would bound either."
  ]
 },
